



/>>



The concept of "/Research Objects/" emerged during the first two decades of 
this century in relation to developments such as the 
observed "/replication crisis/" that began to catch scientists' 
attention at this time %-- specifically, the 
difficulties faced by fellow investigators in reproducing the 
results of (even well-executed) published research work.  A concomitant 
and related notion is "/data transparency/", again related to 
priorities for researchers to document as clearly and openly 
as possible the protocols, data acquisition methods, and 
analytic processes (and assumptions) that underlie published 
findings.  So-called "/Minimum Information Standards/" 
such as MIBBI (Minimum Information for Biological and 
Biomedical Investigations) provide a kind of checklist 
for authors to ensure desired levels and comprehensiveness 
of data transparency.  Simultaneously, open-access 
data sets and deposition of raw data files in 
special-purposes curation sites (Open Science Framework, 
Dataverse, Dryad, etc.) has become commonplace, at 
least in principle.  Publishers often encourage 
(or even require) authors to ensure that raw data is 
available to any one reading research articles, or at 
least to explain explicitly why open data sharing 
is impossible in specific cases.  Some funding 
sources (e.g., the Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation) 
similarly require commit to data transparency as a 
precondition for research teams to have their work supported.  

The term Research Object in itself was apparently coined 
in 2013 to describe a proposed specification for 
how the totality of information related to a scientific 
publication should be packaged.  The underlying idea, 
of course, predates that.  It has become evident for a while 
now that the basic unit of research dissemination is not a 
single document (published article, book, or chapter) 
but rather a collection of text documents, data 
files, potentially analytic code, and (often) 
multimedia materials and graphics; such resources 
collectively should serve as a single entity that 
may be cited, referenced, and downloaded, and users 
should find it straightforward to browse the 
disparate content types and find that information 
or presentations most relevant to them.  The 
Research Object specification is one proposal 
to facilitate these goals, but the general term 
"/research object/" may be used for any package 
that is deliberately structured around data-transparency 
and multi-modal publishing in general (insofar as 
text documents are, in the typical case, understood 
to be accompanied by other content types).

Meanwhile, "/translational science/" has a similar timeline.  
It seems that "/translational medicine/" as a phrase 
first appeared in the 1990's (although the concept is 
older), referencing protocols that 
emphasize practical applications of biomedical research 
and "/bench to bedside/" translation %-- once 
specific clinical interventions or other scientific 
breakthroughs are consolidated in a theoretical 
and/or experimental context, there are then  
further steps needed to deploy novel paradigms 
for concrete benefits.  Almost all scientific research, 
to be sure, is motivated by concern for the social good 
at some level, but the idea behind translational science 
is that self-identified academic focus and specialization 
can augment the probability that practical benefits 
are realized judiciously, in contrast to rather inchoate 
notions of the scientific knowledge's long-term advantages.  
For example, translational scientists have identified 
five general stages in the evolution of health-care 
interventions from abstract ideas to established 
practice %-- the so-called "/translational continuum"/ %-- of which 
the fourth involves commonplace 
adoption of (once-)novel practices in clinical settings 
and the fifth marks concerted efforts to adopt useful interventions in a wide variety of settings (e.g., not restricted to experimental protocols 
available only in a few hospitals, or to a small, select 
population).  Such models suggest roadmaps 
to help guide deployment once research progresses through 
stages of concrete implementation.  Project might unfold 
in roughly this pattern organically, but establishing 
the continuum as a technical model can help the 
progress to advance expeditiously.

Along similar lines, translational science is concerned 
with informatics themes as far as managing data to 
gauge the effectiveness of practical deployments; 
establishing concrete goals and benchmarks as an intrinsic 
dimension of research applicability, plus methods to check 
how empirical results compare to initial projections; 
structuring research to scale up and/or replicate 
concrete implementations; and other ways of 
including practical considerations as intrinsic to the 
foundational research work.  For example, computer 
code and software used (or developed) to analyze 
originary data sets might be repurposed into 
point-of-care modules in concrete deployment contexts.  
As guided by translational concerns, scientists should 
be attentive when setting up and then operating within 
their overall investigatory ecosystem %-- the equipment, 
technology, software, technology, data sources, etc. %-- 
to how such lab/academic settings can be translated to 
operational environments once abstract research progresses 
to concrete, socially-mediated practice.

Although "/transational medicine/" might be the 
most recognizable term, the usefulness of this 
phrasing has extended to other disciplines.  Thus 
we have, for instance, translational chemistry, 
translational engineering, translational sustainability, 
translational humanities, and so forth.  Such 
fields (more or less neatly delineated as subdisciplines) 
focus in on dimensions of their parent specializations 
(chemistry, etc.) that are particularly relevant to 
social improvement, practical benefits, applied 
research, and so forth.  Translational chemistry, 
as a case in point, emphasizes population health 
and ecological issues related to pollution and 
contamination %-- and insofar as principles 
of chemistry clarify the causative mechanisms 
behind compounds' hazards to people and ecosystems 
%-- while also focusing on concrete 
practices to correct social and environmental damage 
(e.g., site remediation) and develop new, better 
forms of molecules and materials (sustainable 
manufacturing, architecture, pharmaceuticals, etc.).  

Translational science is inherently interdisciplinary, 
and often challenges the divisions commonly 
recognized between humanities and natural sciences.  
Theoretical models (such as the translational continuum) aside, 
many of the concrete issues affecting concrete deployments 
are cultural and socioeconomic in nature.  For example, the 
resources and standards of care available in "/inner-city/" 
hospitals differ noticeably from wealthier community's medical 
centers.  Practical challenges when planning 
socially-beneficial implementations of health-care 
ideas driven by new research, as such, derive 
to an important degree from such imbalances in wealth and 
political influence, and the funding, community, and 
institutional infrastructure that can overcome 
such disparities in the translation 
of fortuitous research to operational practice.

It should be obvious that the rationales behind 
Research Objects and Translational Science overlap in 
significant ways.  These specific terms consolidate 
trends in science and academia that are broadly applicable, 
regardless of how scholars involves specifically 
conceputalize them.  A lot of research work is  
motivated by concerns parallel to those of translational 
medicine, whether or not this wording is specifically 
chosen.  Likewise, any author who plans to develop 
a structure set of supplemental materials in consort 
with a single publication (a traditional human-readable 
text document) is confronting challenges that motivate 
the Research Object model.  Moreover, researchers 
committed (explicitly or not) to translational 
principles are likely to structure presentations 
of the work in the multi-modal fashion associated 
with open-access information sharing and data transparency.  
In short, there are strong grounds for formulating 
"/Translational Research Object/" protocols that 
address both of these areas, with application beyond 
just those projects that explicitly invoke 
each one individually. 

Given the socioeconomic issues recently mentioned, 
one might also stipulate that both Translational 
Science and Research Objects reflect an ongoing 
evolution in the assessment of and institutional 
logistics for investigations.  If present trends 
continue, the individual peer-reviewed article 
is likely to lose its status as the pre-eminent 
certificate of merit for individuals as well 
as research projects in general.  This reflects 
both technical and social factors.  On the 
one hand, to the degree that the documentary 
and pedagogical manifestation of research work 
expands outside individual publications to 
encompass data packages and multimedia content, 
the procedures for peer review and assessment 
need to change accordingly.  Evidently, 
common practice (still) is for reviewers to 
evaluate */papers/*, but largely pass over 
data sets, computer code, or GUI materials %-- which 
could in principle represent a more substantial 
authors' time commitment and innovation 
compared to, say, a single journal article itself.  
Meanwhile, the ideal settings for translational 
science are not restricted to academic labs or 
campuses as commonly understood, and the provenance, 
funding, and credentials associated with translational 
research may not fit conventional assessment models.  
For instance, the majority of work and observations 
critical to a paper on public health may have been 
performed by scientists or technicians 
(or, say, social workers) at an inner-city 
hospital, rather than either faculty or students 
at a university.  

Part of translational science's 
entrenchment may, indeed, be the migration 
of academic practices and expertise outside the 
college campus into a broader spectrum of institutions 
and social environments.  This is not to downplay the 
special focus necessary to conduct science on a rigorous, 
academic level.  Specialists with hands-on expertise 
in practical settings may not have the theoretical 
instincts to triangulate the observations or 
day-to-day functioning of practical deployment 
against the abstract structures of a particular 
field if scholarship.  Extra work is needed to 
marshal practical know-how, experience, and information 
into data relevant to theory-confirmation (or disconfirmation).  
At the same time, practitioners of abstract research 
do not necessarily have (or develop) expertise 
forged from everyday practice in institutional/implementational 
settings.  Translational science points to a hybrid 
academic culture that synthesizes elements from both 
on-campus and "/in-the-field/" environments.

As a way of grounding this (itself somewhat abstract) 
discussion, consider the phenomnon of open-source code 
libraries.  GIT repositories, for example, are 
not peer-reviewed, and indeed content available on a platform 
such as Github can vary substantially in quality and interest 
to relevant communities.  Some repos may be essentially 
just some person's schoolwork or experimentation for 
personal use.  On the other hand, some reposities 
created by a single individual are substantial 
resources that deserve attention comparable to an influential 
book or article, and other excellent libraries merge 
the contributions of many experts.  The variegated 
status and purposes of repos does not, in general, prevent 
programmers and scientists from finding open-access code 
relevant to their work.  A quick look at "/readme/" files 
can usually clarify whether a particular repository 
is intended as a serious contribution envision for 
use by multiple third parties, or a work-in-progress toward 
that end, or rather something much smaller.  And in the 
former category a review of the actual code can serve as an 
indication of the project's overall status %-- 
whether it is actively maintained, compatible with current 
compiler and language standards, interopable with other 
code bases, and so forth.  Successful projects often acquire 
their own momentum insofar as additional programmers can add 
their own components or adaptations %-- porting to 
new environments, scripting-language wrappers, adding 
new functionality, etc. %-- thereby expanding the 
scope and usability of the project overall.  Open-source 
libraries (and the coders who create them) thereby acquire a 
hierarchy of status and influence in an emergent manner.  
Notions of merit are not dependent on subjective impressions 
of peer reviewers nor on institutional bureaucracies.

Of course, technical facets of the computing ecosystem play a 
role too.  Object files either compile or don't; 
test suites pass or fail.  A reviewer reading a 
late-stage manuscript draft relies on personal impressions 
as to whether the writing fits in familiar ways into 
the disciplinary contexts which the paper evokes, 
as far as whether terminology, methods, analyses, 
and presuppositions are characteristic of the 
expected audience (or if deviations from those paradigms 
are noted and explained).  Reviewers need to judge whether 
article introduce new ideas and whether these open 
avenues for additional research.  This process is subjective, 
especially insofar as the reviewer is working solely with a 
text document.  An open-source library, conversely, 
can be manipulated more tangibly: one can follow 
documented instructed, compile the sources, run test 
suites, etc., and eventually launch whatever executable 
binaries are produced.  Such hands-on activity offers a lot 
of feedback beyond subjective impressions of code quality.

This comparison between peer-reviewed articles and open-source 
code libraries is relevant to contemporary publishing 
because, one might opine, disseminating research in 
the context of translational science is more like 
maintaining and open-source library than preparing  
a submission for a refereed journal.  Those who 
develop translational-science publications, presumably, 
are often immersed in concrete practice (outside 
the scope of academic institution per se) and seek 
to impose investigatory discipline on the information 
and observations that are implicitly available 
in their logistical environment.  Mining the 
"/ambient/" data that exists in clinical, governmental, or 
nonprofit settings is a different pathway to rigorous 
science that constructing artificial data-acquisition 
locales (e.g., research labs).  Such differences also 
affect the initiation and evolution of research projects.  
A graduate student progressing toward a doctoral 
dissertation, for example, will go through a multi-step 
process of exploring topics, finding an adviser, 
forming a comittee, project approval, and so forth, 
each of which has the result of outsiders molding 
and nudging the eventual work in particular paradigmatic 
directions.  Contrast this to a hypothetical 
social worker or medical humanities specialist who 
works in a clinical or governmental setting and, 
with insights shaped by their day-to-day experience, 
decide to seek out public-access data sets and similar 
empirical resources to help translate their 
emerging (on-the-ground-based) personal perspectives into 
systematic presentations accorded scientific merit.  

Publisher need to be aware of the special status evinced 
by translational and multi-disciplinary research so 
that they can best adopt dissemination practices 
proper to the goals of translational sciences, 
and best support authors and investigators who 
pursue them.  For example, researchers traditionally 
have a personal stake in publications because they 
represent a larger community's endorsement of 
someone's general knowledge and competence as a scientist.  
Having a paper accepted by a high-impact journal 
not only means that other will read one's words, but also 
confers a measure of prestige and recognition.  
In a more translational context %-- where a single 
document is not only the basic unit of dissemination 
%-- considerations are somewhat different.  Multi-faceted 
publications documenting the practical success of an 
innovative project, for instance, are valuable to 
the authors not primarily for the venue's name-recognition 
(with respect to a particular scientist looking for 
future grants, tenure, etc.) but because researcher 
are motivated to continue and expand operations 
on the ground (where funding might come from 
government programs for social good, or nonprofits).  
The typical audience for publications in the latter 
context is not necessarily an acedemic reader 
familiar with a particular document format 
(abstract, similar-research statement, summary of 
findings, etc.) but rather interested 
parties from multiple backgrounds (nonprofit 
administrators, civil servants, software engineers) 
who may be most interested in the nontraditional 
resources of a publication package: data sets, 
computer code, statistical graphics, and so forth.  

All of these points have tangible consequences 
for publishing technology as well.  Translational 
science implies not only an intellectual 
transition to multi-disciplinary research 
but also a physical displacement of the 
scientific environment away from academic 
and laboratory settings and toward on-the-ground 
implementations (plus communities of independent 
researchers).  In terms of digital artifacts, 
this is mirrored in the weight of dissimenation 
shiting from conventonal text documents to 
computational and multimedia artifacts.  At 
one level, this means that a reader seeking to 
learn about a public health, or environmental 
remediation, or government/nonprofit partnership 
initiative (as examples) might look 
toward videos, code libaries, "/story maps"/, or 
other digital resources in addition to 
reading a journal-style article.  At another level, 
the expertise and innovations manifest in research 
projects might be expressed in such "/supplemental/" 
materials more than papers themselves.  Publishing 
software therefore bears the burden of helping 
authors develop such materials to the highest quality 
possible.  The continued funding and deployment 
of projects may depend on the success of content 
such as code libraries, data-acquisition, information 
management, and empirical review all of which 
are unified (in whole or in part) into publication 
packages  

 
   
 

 
       
  

  


   




  










